data.content:
This is a well-known **C# 14 span overload resolution** breaking change.

## What happened

In C# 14 (.NET 10), new built-in conversions from arrays to `Span<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>` cause the compiler to bind `array.Contains(num)` to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains`** (span-based) instead of **`Enumerable.Contains`** (LINQ). Expression trees **cannot represent span-based method calls**, so this throws at runtime.

## How to fix

Force the compiler to pick the `Enumerable.Contains` overload by casting to `IEnumerable<T>` or using `.AsEnumerable()`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the span overload from being selected, keeping the expression tree valid.
